WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:16.410
John: Good morning Hank, it's Monday, January
8th. Sarah and I are about to drive out of

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.779
Asheville, on our way to Birmingham, Alabama.
I would like to congratulate you on successfully

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:25.130
completing the Strawberry Hill challenge,
except that you didn't complete it! Hank,

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.300
as you know, I had surgery on my mouth a few
weeks ago. After they sewed my gum back into

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.840
my mouth, my mouth tasted like cement and
blood and burnt meat. And you know how after

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:41.940
any dental procedure they make you drink that,
like, green, gross, minty alcoholy stuff and

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:48.370
then spit? Strawberry Hill tastes exactly
like that spit. Project idea: the Brotherhood

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:55.120
2.0 Book Club. I'll read a book you suggest
if you read a book I suggest. My first suggestion?

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.300
Either "What is the What," by Dave Eggers
or "We Wish to Inform You That Tomorrow We

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.600
Will be Killed With Our Families," by Philip
Gourevitch.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.430
Hank: Hello, John. It's Tuesday, January 9th.
I'm in Detroit, Michigan. I'm standing in

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.710
the only place in the continental United States
where you can look south and see Canada, which

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:19.659
you are doing right now. I'm also standing
in front of General Motors headquarters. It's

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:28.920
a very, very big building. I've excepted an
invitation from General Motors to visit the

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.070
North American International Auto Show in
Detroit, Michigan. I was invited to this because

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.650
I write for a few environmental blogs and
there are some environmental things going

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:46.820
on here. Those are my values. This is not
my values. And here we have the weirdest,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:02:02.210
prettiest fake waterfall ever and it spells
things. So lame. [Girls]: Hi John! Hey John!

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:13.790
See what you're missing? [Hank]: Ehhehehehehehehehehe.
As for the Brotherhood 2.0 book club, I'm

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.280
really excited that you're giving me opportunities
to read so many books about genocide. I love

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.280
genocide.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:24.700
John: Good morning Hank, it's Thur- what?
It's Wednesday, January 10th. Boy, I'm out

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.560
of it. Hi girls who are being used as objects
to sell other objects! Sorry about the patriarchal

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.170
oppression. I was inspired by your video blog
yesterday to calculate my own carbon footprint,

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:41.390
so, um, I'm gonna go to BP.com and do it.
Hank, I produce 16 tons of carbon a year.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:47.520
That is lower than the United States average
of 18.58, but it's still really bad. Sixteen

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.590
tons of carbon. I just read on the internet
that it only costs six dollars per ton to

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:57.150
get rid of your carbon footprint. Uhh, that
means that it's only gonna cost me, like,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:03.069
I'm not very good at math, hold on, I'm gonna
call Daniel. Hey, how's it going? What's sixteen

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:08.930
times six? It's 96. Do you see how fast he
did that? He doesn't have a calculator. $96

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:14.530
a year, that's what it would cost to offset
my carbon consumption. If I pay Al Gore $96,

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:21.810
I will not have any global warming attached
to my name. I think you can pay him directly,

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:26.379
I think you just write him a check, and he
just takes care of it. He does, he eats the

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:29.879
carbon. That's exactly right, that's how he
does it, he eats the carbon. And then when

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:32.239
he poops it out, it's actually political gold.

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:39.379
Hank: Good afternoon, John. It is Thursday,
January 11th. (sneezes twice) So I was just

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:44.510
watching my video from January 9th. I'm paying
the price for not being commensurate with

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:48.769
my values. I don't actually remember this
tiny chicken but I'm almost positive that

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:54.620
we had an encounter, because this tiny chicken
has laid tiny eggs in my head. And the baby

00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:02.830
tiny chickens are feeding on my brain. They're
eating my brain and their poop is coming out

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.060
my nose. I've got a little bit of a problem,
and I was hoping that you or maybe one of

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.930
our viewers could help me out. I was going
to Detroit, right, so I figured I'd have a

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:14.490
bunch of business cards printed up. I used
an online service, and I upload my files,

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:21.169
and they sent me a bunch of business cards.
I like that. I like, it's like a nice, sturdy

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:26.470
card stock. And on the back, it's supposed
to be like this one, which I got printed up

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:35.860
at Kinko's. But this one, on the back, says
this. And on the front, says this. What am

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:43.410
I supposed to do with this? John, viewers:
What do I do with 100 three inch by one-and-a-half

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:48.570
inch cards that say 'ecogeek' on them? So
far my only idea is to tape them all over

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:56.340
my face. (sings) 'I would walk 500 miles and
I would walk 500 more.' See, this is — this

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.340
is to me not a punishment. I— I don't know.
To me, it's not a punishment. We sh— we

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.590
need to come up with better punishments, is
what I'm saying.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.770
John: Good morning Hank; it's Friday, January
12th. This video blog post is going to consist

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:12.699
entirely of ideas. Misprinted business card
utilization ideas: if you had a hole punch,

00:05:12.699 --> 00:05:16.680
a lot of patience, and a lot of dental floss,
you could make Katherine a dress. Since you

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.970
just got married, you can use them to write
very terse thank-you notes in very small handwriting.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:25.690
You could wallpaper every room in your dollhouse.
Everybody thinks you have a dollhouse now!

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.360
Punishment ideas: I'm just going to pick punishment
ideas from things that are currently in our

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.919
living room. Several of our books would make
excellent punishments. This is The Continuum

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:43.130
History of Apocalypticism. It's eleven-hundred
pages long. It's pretty punishing. Don't you

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.300
think it would be a good punishment if you
had to do your Happy Dance for three minutes

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.889
and thirty seconds, but you could only do
it at five second clips at a time, so you

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:59.900
had to have a total of 70 dances in 70 different
locations? That'd be a pretty good punishment.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:05.720
Or maybe that should be a project? Maybe we
could intersperse our Happy Dances. You do

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.879
35, I do 35. That has some appeal for me,
any interest?

